        "title": "Introduction",
        "key_prerequisites": [],
        "has_content_in_snapshot": true,
        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To raise a fraction to a power, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power. This is known as the <strong>power of quotient rule for exponents</strong>.</p>",
            "readable_text": "To raise a fraction to a power, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power. This is known as the power of quotient rule for exponents.",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To demonstrate, let&#x27;s simplify the expression <span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3]</span> by applying the power of quotient rule:</p>",
            "readable_text": "To demonstrate, let's simplify the expression [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3] by applying the power of quotient rule:",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><strong>Note:</strong> The power of quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: ((2)/(5))^{3}|= ((2)/(5)) \u00d7 ((2)/(5)) \u00d7 ((2)/(5)); = (2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2)/(5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5); = (2^{3})/(5^{3})]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Note: The power of quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n[MATH: ((2)/(5))^{3}|= ((2)/(5)) \u00d7 ((2)/(5)) \u00d7 ((2)/(5)); = (2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2)/(5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5); = (2^{3})/(5^{3})]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nTo raise a fraction to a power, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power. This is known as the power of quotient rule for exponents.\n\nContent:\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3] by applying the power of quotient rule:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}]\n\nContent:\nNote: The power of quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n[MATH: ((2)/(5))^{3}|= ((2)/(5)) \u00d7 ((2)/(5)) \u00d7 ((2)/(5)); = (2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2)/(5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5); = (2^{3})/(5^{3})]"
      },
