    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo raise a fraction to a power, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power. This is known as the power of quotient rule for exponents.\n\nContent:\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the expression [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3] by applying the power of quotient rule:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{2^3}{5^3}]\n\nContent:\nNote: The power of quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to using repeated multiplication, as follows:\n[MATH: ((2)/(5))^{3}|= ((2)/(5)) \u00d7 ((2)/(5)) \u00d7 ((2)/(5)); = (2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2)/(5 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 5); = (2^{3})/(5^{3})]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Applying the Power of Quotient Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)^2] as the quotient of two powers.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo raise the fraction to the power of [MATH: 2,] we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of [MATH: 2\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)^2=\\dfrac{3^2}{4^2}]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((3)/(8))^{7} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (21)/(56)]\nb. [MATH: (3^{7})/(8)]\nc. [MATH: (3^{7})/(8^{7})]\nd. [MATH: (3)/(8^{7})]\ne. [MATH: (21)/(8)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (4 \u00f7 11)^{3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 4^{3} \u00f7 11^{3}]\nb. [MATH: 4^{3} \u00f7 11]\nc. [MATH: 4 \u00f7 11^{3}]\nd. [MATH: 4 \u00f7 (11 \u00d7 3)]\ne. [MATH: 4 \u00f7 (11 + 3)]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Evaluating Powers of Fractions Using Power of Quotient Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^3.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo raise the fraction to the power of [MATH: 3,] we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of [MATH: 3\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: \\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{4^3}{5^3}]\n\nNow, computing [MATH: 4^3=64] and [MATH: 5^3=125,] we get\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{4^3}{5^3}= \\dfrac{64}{125} .]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 6((2)/(3))^{3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (8)/(9)]\nb. [MATH: (2)/(9)]\nc. [MATH: (16)/(9)]\nd. [MATH: (8)/(27)]\ne. [MATH: (16)/(27)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((3)/(5))^{2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (9)/(25)]\nb. [MATH: (3)/(10)]\nc. [MATH: (9)/(5)]\nd. [MATH: (3)/(25)]\ne. [MATH: (6)/(5)]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Evaluating Powers of Negative Fractions Using the Power of Quotient Rule\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: \\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^3.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, notice that [MATH: -\\dfrac{3}{2}=\\dfrac{(-3)}{2}.]\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of [MATH: 3], we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of [MATH: 3\\mathbin{:}]\n\n[MATH: \\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^3=\\left(\\dfrac{(-3)}{2}\\right)^3=\\dfrac{(-3)^3}{2^3}]\n\nNow, computing [MATH: (-3)^3=-27] and [MATH: 2^3=8,] we get\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{(-3)^3}{2^3}= \\dfrac{-27}{8}=-\\dfrac{27}{8} .]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 (5)/(4))^{3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 (25)/(16)]\nb. [MATH: (25)/(16)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (125)/(64)]\nd. [MATH: (125)/(64)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (15)/(12)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 (1)/(7))^{2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (1)/(7)]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(49)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(49)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(14)]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(7)]"
